<<<
Elements of English Com
===
*Elements of English Com
>>>

<<<
position, 11th Ed. 1841) To imple
===
position*, 11th Ed. 1841) To *imple
>>>

<<<
me’nt, signifying to fulfil, is likewise
===
me’nt*, signifying to fulfil, is likewise
>>>

<<<
Scotish (sic) bar.
===
Scotish (*sic*) bar.
>>>

<<<
implicate makes -cable; see -ABLE 1.
===
**implicate** makes *-cable*; see ^^-ABLE^^ 1.
>>>

<<<
implicit. I.)(explicit; %.)(implied; i. faith &c.
===
**implicit.** *I.*)(*explicit*; *i.*)(*implied* i. *faith* &c.
>>>

<<<
one of these contrasts as egplicit & implicit, & then dash
===
one of these contrasts as *explicit* & *implicit*, & then dash
>>>

<<<
say in ‘explicit support’ & ‘implicit obedience’, with absolute or complete or full as a substitute
===
say in ‘*explicit support*’ & ‘*implicit obedience*’, with *absolute* or *complete* or *full* as a substitute
>>>

<<<
perhaps decide that implicit is a shifty word
===
perhaps decide that *implicit* is a shifty word
>>>

<<<
the same as implied, &, as it is
===
the same as *implied*, &, as it is
>>>

<<<
by the side of implied is perhaps due
===
by the side of *implied* is perhaps due
>>>

<<<
one is that explicit & implicit make a neater antithesis than even egpressed & implied (all the conditions whether explicit or implicit; but all the implied conditions; implied is much commoner than implicit when the antithesis is not given in full);
===
one is that *explicit* & *implicit* make a neater antithesis than even *expressed* & *implied* (*all the conditions whether explicit or implicit; but all the implied conditions; implied is much commoner than implicit when the antithesis is not given in full*);
>>>

<<<
whether of implicit or of implied, is more often wanted
===
whether of *implicit* or of *implied*, is more often wanted
>>>

<<<
& that impliedly is felt to be (see -EDLY 5) a bad form; implicitly, preferred to impliedly, helps to keep implicit alive.
===
& that *impliedly* is felt to be (see ^^-EDLY^^ 5) a bad form; *implicitly*, preferred to *impliedly*, helps to keep *implicit* alive.
>>>

<<<
accident by which implicit, with faith, obedience, confidence, & such words, has come to mean absolute or full, whereas its original sense was undeveloped or potential or in the germ, The starting-point
===
accident by which *implicit*, with *faith*, *obedience*, *confidence*, & such words, has come to mean absolute or full, whereas its original sense was undeveloped or potential or in the germ. The starting-point
>>>

<<<
+ implicit faith, i.e. a person’s acceptance
===
+ *implicit faith*, i.e. a person’s acceptance
>>>

<<<
See POPULARIZED TECHNICALITIES.
===
See ^^POPULARIZED TECHNICALITIES^^.
>>>

<<<
impliedly is a bad form; see -EDLY

5
===
**impliedly** is a bad form; see ^^-EDLY^^ (5).
>>>

<<<
imply. For inflexions see VERES IN -IE, -Y, -VE, 6.
===
**imply.** For inflexions see ^^VERBS IN -IE, -Y, -YE^^, 6.
>>>

<<<
impoliti¢ makes the unusual adverbial form -icly, not -ically.
===
**impolitic** makes the unusual adverbial form *-icly*, not *-ically*.
>>>

<<<
import. See Noun & VERB ACCENT.
===
**import.** See ^^NOUN^^ & ^^VERB ACCENT^^.
>>>

<<<
importune, v. The stress is variable
===
**importune**, v. The stress is variable
>>>

<<<
impose makes -sable; see MUTE E.
===
**impose** makes *-sable*; see ^^MUTE E^^.
>>>

<<<
impost. For synonymy see TAX.
===
**impost.** For synonymy see ^^TAX^^.
>>>

<<<
impost(h)ume. The k, which is not pronounced
===
**impost(h)ume.** The *h*, which is not pronounced
>>>

<<<
It should be, & was, apostem, from Greek apostema abscess; the h comes in by confusion with posthumous, in which
===
It should be, & was, *apostem*, from Greek *apostema* abscess; the *h* comes in by confusion with *posthumous*, in which
>>>

<<<
impractical, un-.

The second is better; see IN- & UN-, & PRAC- TICABLE.
===
**impractical, un-.** The second is better; see ^^IN-^^ & ^^UN-^^, & ^^PRACTICABLE^^.
>>>

<<<
The constant confusion between practicable & practical is a special reason
===
The constant confusion between *practicable* & *practical* is a special reason
>>>

<<<
in the negatives: Its inability to address itself to the questions of the hour produces the impression that the Labour movement is all impracticable agitation (read unpractical),
===
in the negatives: *Its inability to address itself to the questions of the hour produces the impression that the Labour movement is all impracticable agitation* (read *unpractical*).
>>>
